==English==
{{was wotd|2006|May|23}}

===Etymology===
Origin uncertain; compare (probably from<ref>[http://www.etymonline.com/index.php?term=simper simper] in [[:w:Online Etymology dictionary|Online Etymology dictionary]]</ref>) {{der|en|da|simper}} / {{m|da|semper||[[coy]]}}, German {{m|de|zimper||elegant, dainty}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|en|/ˈsɪmpə/}}
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|en|/ˈsɪmpɚ/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-simper.ogg|Audio (US)}}
*: {{rhymes|en|ɪmpə(r)}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[smile]] in a [[foolish]], [[frivolous]], [[self-conscious]], [[coy]], or [[smug]] manner.
#* '''1892''', {{w|Mark Twain}}, ''{{w|The American Claimant}}'', ch. 21:
#*: Why, look at him—look at this '''simpering''' self-righteous mug!
#* '''1915''', {{w|Harold MacGrath}}, ''The Voice In The Fog'', ch. 24:
#*: How the fools kotowed and '''simpered''' while I looked over their jewels and speculated upon how much I could get for them!
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1986|author={{w|John le Carré}}| title={{w|A Perfect Spy}}| passage= But where daft Nell '''simpers''' at him and tries to muss his slicked hair and pull it forward over his broad, Christian brow, my little Dot is looking nowhere but at the ground, still praying, praying even while she stands, and Rick has actually to touch her forearm with his finger in order to alert her to his Godlike nearness.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} To glimmer; to twinkle.
#* '''1633''', {{w|George Herbert}}, ''The Search''
#*: Yet can I mark how stars above / '''Simper''' and shine.

